                "The mathematical core of the Arbitrage Detection Engine is the Dynamic Landed Arbitrage Threshold (DLAT). For any given product SKU across any pairwise combination of geographic markets (Source Market i, Destination Market j), the engine calculates the net economic profit margin available to an unauthorized transshipper in real time. The model ingests live spot foreign exchange rates, local retail and wholesale price indices, statutory tariff schedules (such as Harmonized Tariff Schedule HTS codes), international freight shipping rates, value-added tax (VAT) reclaim rules, and marketplace seller commission fees. When the predicted arbitrage yield exceeds the transshipper's hurdle rate (typically 12% to 18% net margin), the system flags the corridor as High Risk of Imminent Diversion, prompting automatic recommendations to adjust regional wholesale prices or implement distributor allocation caps.",
